                    "Value": {
                      "StringWithMarkup": [
                        {
                          "String": "The effects of amino acids on the embryotoxicity and placental transfer of nickel chloride, day 10 rat embryos were cultured in rat serum medium containing nickel chloride or NiCl2-63 (0.34 or 0.68 uM NiCl with or without L-histidine (2 uM), L-aspartic acid, glycine (2 or 8 uM) or L-cysteine (2 uM). After 26 hr, conceptuses were assessed for survival, growth and development and malformations. The nickel-63 contents of embryos and yolk sacs and the extent of Nickel-63 binding to the proteins of the culture medium were also determined. Nickel chloride alone did not affect the embryonic development at 0.34 uM and caused growth retardation and brain and caudal abnormalities at 0.68 uM. Coincubation of L-histidine, L-cysteine, or L-aspartic acid 0.68 uM Ni reduced the growth retardation and the incidence and/or severity of brain defects caused by nickel chloride and decreased the concentrations of nickel-63 in the yolk sacs compared to 0.68 uM nickel-63 alone. In the presence of L-histidine, L-cysteine or L-aspartic acid there was a shift of nickel-63 binding from the high molecular weight proteins of the culture medium to the low molecular weight fraction.",
